    meta(name="apple-mobile-web-app-status-bar-style" content="black-translucent")

    //- Social / SEO. Without these the page unfurls as a bare URL with no
    //- title card anywhere it gets shared.
    meta(name="description" content="How did Americans really vote? A scrollytelling look at US presidential results by county, 2000-2024, showing why maps of land mislead us about votes.")
    meta(name="author" content="John Alexis Guerra Gomez")
    meta(name="keywords" content="US elections, data visualization, county results, d3, scrollytelling, presidential election, cartogram")
    link(rel="canonical" href="https://johnguerra.co/viz/USElectionsResults/")

    meta(property="og:type" content="article")
    meta(property="og:site_name" content="johnguerra.co")
    meta(property="og:url" content="https://johnguerra.co/viz/USElectionsResults/")
    meta(property="og:title" content="US Presidential Election Results, 2000-2024")
    meta(property="og:description" content="Land does not vote, people do. A scrollytelling look at US presidential results by county, from a map to a beeswarm of 3,100 counties sized by voters.")
    meta(property="og:image" content="https://johnguerra.co/viz/USElectionsResults/img/og.png")
    meta(property="og:image:width" content="1200")
    meta(property="og:image:height" content="630")
    meta(property="og:image:alt" content="Thousands of circles, one per US county, coloured red and blue and spread left to right by which way the county voted.")

    meta(name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image")
    meta(name="twitter:creator" content="@duto_guerra")
    meta(name="twitter:title" content="US Presidential Election Results, 2000-2024")
    meta(name="twitter:description" content="Land does not vote, people do. A scrollytelling look at US presidential results by county.")
    meta(name="twitter:image" content="https://johnguerra.co/viz/USElectionsResults/img/og.png")

    link(rel="preconnect", href="https://fonts.googleapis.com")
    link(rel="preconnect", href="https://fonts.gstatic.com",crossorigin)
    link(href="https://fonts.googleapis.com/css2?family=Fjalla+One&family=Old+Standard+TT:ital,wght@0,400;0,700;1,400&family=PT+Sans+Narrow:wght@400;700&display=swap",rel="stylesheet")


    link(rel="stylesheet" href="./lib/css/bootstrap.min.css")

    link(rel="stylesheet", href="./css/main.css")
    link(rel="stylesheet" type="text/css" href="css/scroller.css")
  body
    .container

      span.d-flex.flex-row-reverse
        <a class="github-button" href="https://github.com/john-guerra/USPresidentialElectionVisualization" data-color-scheme="no-preference: dark; light: light; dark: dark;" data-icon="octicon-star" data-size="large" aria-label="Star john-guerra/USPresidentialElectionVisualization on GitHub">Star</a>


      .row
        .col-md-12.col-xs-12
          h1 US Presidential Election Results


      //- Year picker: a fixed pill that stays reachable on every viewport.
      //- The odometer is the visual; the native select sits transparently on
      //- top of it so phones still get their own picker UI and a11y for free.
      #yearPicker
        span#yearPickerLabel Year
        #yearOdometer(aria-hidden="true")
        svg#yearPickerCaret(viewBox="0 0 10 6" aria-hidden="true")
          path(d="M1 1l4 4 4-4" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.5" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round")
        select#yearSelect(aria-labelledby="yearPickerLabel")
          option(value="2024", selected) 2024
          option(value="2020") 2020
          option(value="2016") 2016
          option(value="2012") 2012
          option(value="2008") 2008
          option(value="2004") 2004
          option(value="2000") 2000

      //- Progress rail: 24 dots, one per story step, click to jump.
      nav#progress(aria-label="Story progress")

      #graphic.row
        #vis
          div(style="display:none;")
            img(id="imgTitle" src="img/title.png" width="100px")
          div#vTooltip.col-xs-12.col-md-4
            h3 Tooltip
            p content
            div#barChart




        div.col-md-8#visFiller
        #sections.col-xs-12.col-md-4
          section.step(data-step-id="americans-vote-presidential")
            h2.title How did Americans vote in the #[span.yearValue] Presidential Election?
            p This is an analysis of the voting in the counties of the United States in the presidential #[span.yearValue] election.
            strong#scrollHint.scroll-hint
              | Scroll down to continue.
              span.scroll-hint-arrow(aria-hidden="true") ↓

          section.step(data-step-id="visualize-results")
            h2.title Let's visualize the results!
            p But how? Maybe with a map of the United States...

          section.step(data-step-id="add-colors")
            h2.title Let's add colors
            p We can use colors to differentiate who won in each state. If a state voted mostly for #[span.colorDemocrat Democrats] it will be #[span.colorDemocrat blue], if it voted mostly for #[span.colorRepublican Republicans] it will be #[span.colorRepublican red].

          section.step(data-step-id="really-red")
            h2.title So is the US really that red?
            p One of the problems with this graphic is that it does not show the individual results of the counties.

          section.step(data-step-id="results-county")
            h2.title Results by county
            p By counties the result is different, but also misleading, but it is still far from accurate as it doesn't represent the actual percentage of votes...

          section.step(data-step-id="use-color-saturation")
            h2.title Let's use color saturation
            p Let's add a scale where darker colors represent higher differences, and lighter colors represent smaller differences. However, this is still misleading...


          section.step(data-step-id="geographic-area-misleading")
            h2.title Geographic area is misleading
            p For example, even though #[span#exampleLeft] and #[span#exampleRight] have similar opposing results percent wise, they are very different in terms of population (#[span#exampleLeftPopulation] vs #[span#exampleRightPopulation]).


          section.step(data-step-id="number-voters")
            h2.title What about the number of voters?
            p Another problem with this map is that it shows the results by geographic area, and for example, rural counties have a lot of area but a small population.

          section.step(data-step-id="circle-each-county")
            h2.title A circle for each county
            p Let's replace each county with a circle of the same size to make a more accurate visual comparison.

          section.step(data-step-id="dont-fit")
            h2.title They don't fit!
            p Another problem, the circles appear on top of each other 🤦🏻‍♂️...

          section.step(data-step-id="respect-my-bubble")
            h2.title Respect my bubble!
            p Let's add a repulsion force that makes each circle try to avoid being on top of another.

          section.step(data-step-id="number-voters-2")
            h2.title But what about the number of voters?
            p We still have the problem that all circles are the same size ☹️. How about we use the size of the circle...

          section.step(data-step-id="size-matters-case")
            h2.title Size matters (in this case)
            p How about we use the #[strong area] (not the radius) of the circles to represent the total number of voters.

          section.step(data-step-id="details")
            h2.title Details
            p If you want to know the values of each county, move the mouse over the center of the circle.

          section.step(data-step-id="compare-better")
            h2.title Now we can compare better
            p With this map it is a bit easier to see how the country voted.

          section.step(data-step-id="patterns-regions")
            h2.title Patterns by regions
            p For example, it can be seen how larger counties and near the coast tend to vote more for #[span.colorDemocrat Democrats], while interior counties for #[span.colorRepublican Republicans].

          section.step(data-step-id="overall-result")
            h2.title What about the overall result?
            p With the map it is not so easy to know. (In reality, maps are not the best way to visualize data for most analysis tasks 😉).

          section.step(data-step-id="remove-map")
            h2.title Let's remove the map then...
            p Now we have more space to use. But the counties are still located by geographic position, which is no longer necessary.

          section.step(data-step-id="everyone-middle")
            h2.title Everyone to the middle
            p Removing the geographic position allows us to use the location to encode more information. For example, we can place counties horizontally depending on who the voted more towards...

          section.step(data-step-id="left-right")
            h2.title From left to right
            p To differentiate the counties more easily, we can distribute them horizontally by how they voted. We send to the #[span.colorDemocrat left] those who voted more for #[span.colorDemocrat Democrats] and to the #[span.colorRepublican right] those who voted more for #[span.colorRepublican Republicans].

          section.step(data-step-id="vertical-location")
            h2.title What about the vertical location?
            p We can use it to divide by regions.

          section.step(data-step-id="voting-regions")
            h2.title Voting by Regions
            p This graphic shows us how each region of the United States voted. Here we can observe, for example, how the New England region tends to vote more for #[span.colorDemocrat Democrats], versus East South Central which voted more for #[span.colorRepublican Republicans]. This allows for easier comparison and understanding of the final election results.

          section.step(data-step-id="voting-population")
            h2.title Voting by population
            p We can also change the vertical position to encode the number of voters by population.

          #extra-space
          section.step.last(data-step-id="thank-you")
            h2.title Thank you!
            p Thanks for making it this far, I hope you liked it. If you have any questions or suggestion feel free to reach out via social media, just search for #[a(href="http://johnguerra.co") John Alexis Guerra Gómez].
            br
            p Using data from #[a(href="https://dataverse.harvard.edu/dataset.xhtml?persistentId=doi:10.7910/DVN/VOQCHQ") MIT Election Data and Science Lab]
            blockquote.
              MIT Election Data and Science Lab, 2018, "County Presidential Election Returns 2000-2020", https://doi.org/10.7910/DVN/VOQCHQ, Harvard Dataverse, V13, UNF:6:GILlTHRWH0LbH2TItBsb2w== [fileUNF]
            p 2024 Data from #[a(href="https://github.com/tonmcg/US_County_Level_Election_Results_08-24") https://github.com/tonmcg/US_County_Level_Election_Results_08-24]


          #extra-space
          #extra-space




